    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter May 11th with a focused offensive strategy, prioritizing high-upside spots against weaker pitching rotations. **Junior Caminero** leads the charge with a 67.23 score; despite a respectable 3.72 xERA from Gausman, the combination of a favorable park factor and a premium #2 lineup slot makes **Junior Caminero** our primary engine for Runs and RBI. Close behind, **Yordan Alvarez** warrants a strong start (64.21) despite facing a formidable arm in Kirby. While Kirby\u2019s skill level drags down the projection, **Yordan Alvarez**\u2019s elite 0.429 xwOBA and his placement in the two-hole keep his efficiency well within our \"Strong\" start threshold. Meanwhile, **Casey Schmitt** (63.20) benefits from a significant matchup exploit, facing Sasaki\u2019s 5.30 xERA; the decision to bat him third provides a massive boost to his ceiling, making him our highest-leveraged play against a struggling pitcher.\n\nRounding out the lineup, we are exercising caution with **Kazuma Okamoto** (49.61) and **Luke Raley** (52.38). **Kazuma Okamoto** faces Rasmussen\u2019s 3.67 xERA, and despite the positive park factor, the platoon disadvantage against a righty keeps his utility score firmly in the \"Marginal\" territory. **Hyeseong Kim** (40.69) serves as our lineup anchor, though his grade is tempered by a difficult matchup against McDonald\u2019s elite 2.60 xERA; the optimizer keeps him active purely for roster continuity, though he sits near the bottom of our efficiency floor. With **Pete Crow-Armstrong**, **Trea Turner**, and **Kyle Schwarber** sidelined due to team off-days and **Christian Yelich** remaining on the IL, our depth is severely constrained. We are closely monitoring weather reports for the **Casey Schmitt** matchup, as any rain delay could turn his favorable hitting conditions into a slog, though for now, he remains our most efficient play on the board.",
